### What are the standard detail types?

| Detail Type | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `ErrorInfo` | Reason string, domain, key-value metadata |
| `BadRequest` | Per-field violations for form validation |
| `RetryInfo` | Minimum delay before retrying |
| `QuotaFailure` | Which quota was exceeded |
| `PreconditionFailure` | Which precondition failed |
| `ResourceInfo` | Which resource was involved |
| `DebugInfo` | Stack trace (internal use only) |

### What are common mistakes with gRPC status codes?

The first mistake is using INTERNAL as a catch-all for every error, which hides client errors that should be INVALID_ARGUMENT or NOT_FOUND. The second is leaking sensitive internal details in the status description.

```java
// WRONG: INTERNAL for a client error
observer.onError(
    Status.INTERNAL
        .withDescription("Account not found")
        .asRuntimeException());
// Should be NOT_FOUND, not INTERNAL

// WRONG: leaking internal details
observer.onError(
    Status.INTERNAL
        .withDescription("SQL error: ORA-12154 on db-prod-03")
        .asRuntimeException());
// The client can read this!

// CORRECT: appropriate code, safe description
observer.onError(
    Status.NOT_FOUND
        .withDescription("Account SAV2001 not found")
        .asRuntimeException());
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Use the standard detail types rather than inventing custom ones. Keep INTERNAL strictly for genuine server bugs. Never leak sensitive internal information in the description. Use ErrorInfo with a reason and domain so clients can handle errors programmatically without parsing message strings.
